<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Baudelaire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Charles-Pierre Baudelaire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Baudelaire was a French poet, essayist, translator and art critic, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. His collection Les Fleurs du mal (The Flowers of Evil), published in 1857, dealt with sex, death and the changing city; he, his publisher and the printer were prosecuted for an offence against public morals, six poems were suppressed, and the judgment was reversed only in 1949. He translated stories by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edgar-allan-poe">Edgar Allan Poe</a>, coined the term modernité for the fleeting experience of urban life, and influenced Paul Verlaine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-rimbaud">Arthur Rimbaud</a> and Stéphane Mallarmé. After a stroke in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1866 he was left paralysed and died in Paris in 1867.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-baudelaire.jpg" alt="Charles Baudelaire" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Notwist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baudelaire_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Baudelaire was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 9 April 1821. His father, Joseph-François Baudelaire, a senior civil servant and amateur artist, was 34 years older than his mother, Caroline Dufaÿs, and died in 1827. The next year Caroline married Lieutenant Colonel Jacques Aupick, later a French ambassador. Baudelaire wrote to his mother that &quot;There was in my childhood a period of passionate love for you.&quot;</p><p>He studied law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and began to run up debts. After gaining his degree in 1839 he told his brother, &quot;I don't feel I have a vocation for anything.&quot; In 1841 his stepfather sent him on a voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> to end his dissolute habits, but he left the ship after a few weeks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> and Réunion. At 21 he received a sizable inheritance, which he squandered, and his family placed his property in trust.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first published work, under the pseudonym Baudelaire Dufaÿs, was the art review &quot;Salon of 1845&quot;; in 1846 he wrote a second, championing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugene-delacroix">Eugène Delacroix</a> as the foremost Romantic painter. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a> and wrote for a revolutionary newspaper, though he later noted that his interest in politics was passing. From 1847 until 1865 he was largely occupied with translating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edgar-allan-poe">Edgar Allan Poe</a>, and the translations were widely praised.</p><p>Les Fleurs du mal appeared in 1857. Théodore de Banville described its effect on fellow artists as &quot;immense, prodigious, unexpected&quot;, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustave-flaubert">Gustave Flaubert</a> wrote that Baudelaire had found a way to rejuvenate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>, while J. Habas wrote in Le Figaro that everything in it not hideous was incomprehensible. A second edition without the six condemned poems but with considerable additions appeared in 1861, and the six were printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1866 as Les Épaves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1864 Baudelaire left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, hoping to sell the rights to his works and give lectures. He suffered a massive stroke in 1866 and spent his last year semi-paralysed in nursing homes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he died on 31 August 1867. A few dozen people attended his funeral, and he is buried in the Cimetière du Montparnasse.</p><p>Four years after his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-rimbaud">Arthur Rimbaud</a> called him &quot;the king of poets, a true God&quot;. In 1895 Stéphane Mallarmé published a sonnet in his memory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-s-eliot">T. S. Eliot</a> said he had learnt his art &quot;under the aegis of Baudelaire&quot;, and Walter Benjamin made him the focus of his Das Passagenwerk.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Baudelaire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Clement Greenberg placed it in mid-19th-century France, with <b>Charles Baudelaire</b> in literature and Manet in painting; William Everdell dated it to the 1870s, with Richard Dedekind’s mathematics and Ludwig Boltzmann’s statistical thermodynamics, and in painting to Georges Seurat’s Divisionism of 1885–1886.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
